# kagent on EKS — configuration
#
# setup-model.sh, install-kagent.sh, make-lean-agent.sh and uninstall.sh all
# read this file. (bootstrap-eks.sh does not -- it takes its settings from
# the command line, see below.) Edit it once, before you start.
#
# ============================================================================
# NO SECRETS IN THIS FILE.
#
# The Azure OpenAI API key is never stored here. setup-model.sh asks for it,
# types it invisibly, and puts it straight into a Kubernetes Secret. Putting it
# in this file would leak it into `helm get values` output, into your shell
# history, and into the process list where any user on the machine can read it.
# ============================================================================


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The cluster
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Name of the EKS cluster. bootstrap-eks.sh does NOT read this file, so if you
# want a different name you must pass it on the command line as well:
#
#     CLUSTER_NAME="my-cluster" ./bootstrap-eks.sh
#
# The value here is what uninstall.sh asks you to type back as confirmation.
CLUSTER_NAME="kagent-rehearsal"

# AWS region. Must match the region your sandbox or account gives you --
# creating in the wrong region is the most common early mistake. Like
# CLUSTER_NAME, bootstrap-eks.sh reads this from the environment, not from
# here: AWS_REGION="eu-west-1" ./bootstrap-eks.sh
AWS_REGION="us-east-1"

# THE SAFETY GUARD. uninstall.sh refuses to delete anything unless the cluster
# your kubectl is currently pointed at matches this exactly.
#
# LEAVE THIS BLANK UNTIL bootstrap-eks.sh HAS RUN. The script prints the correct
# value at the end -- copy it here verbatim.
#
# It looks like this (a full ARN, not a short name):
#   arn:aws:eks:us-east-1:123456789012:cluster/kagent-rehearsal
#
# If you have more than one cluster in your kubeconfig, this line is the only
# thing standing between a typo and deleting the wrong one. Never blank it out
# to "make the error go away".
EXPECTED_CONTEXT=""


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Where kagent goes
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# kagent gets its own namespace. Nothing else on the cluster is touched.
KAGENT_NAMESPACE="kagent"


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The model — Azure OpenAI
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# kagent needs a chat model that supports TOOL CALLING (also called function
# calling). It does nothing but call tools, so a model without it will install
# perfectly and then answer every question in prose, which looks like a broken
# product. setup-model.sh proves this before anything is installed.
#
# The model does NOT have to be in AWS. It is reached over the internet with an
# API key, so an Azure OpenAI deployment works fine from an EKS cluster.

# The base hostname of your Azure OpenAI resource. Must be the bare host with a
# trailing slash -- NOT a full request URL.
#
#   right:  https://my-resource.openai.azure.com/
#   wrong:  https://my-resource.services.ai.azure.com/openai/v1/responses
#
# Azure AI Foundry shows you the second form. It will not work: it is a
# different API with a different request shape, and this toolkit speaks the
# classic chat/completions API. If your Foundry resource is called `my-resource`,
# the value you want is https://my-resource.openai.azure.com/
AOAI_ENDPOINT="https://YOUR-RESOURCE.openai.azure.com/"

# Deployment name and model name are INDEPENDENT and both are required.
#
#   AOAI_DEPLOYMENT  the name YOU chose when creating the deployment in Azure.
#                    It goes in the request URL.
#   AOAI_MODEL       the underlying model the deployment serves. It goes into
#                    kagent's ModelConfig.
#
# They are often the same, because people name deployments after the model.
# Check both in the Azure portal under your resource -> Model deployments.
AOAI_DEPLOYMENT="your-deployment-name"
AOAI_MODEL="your-model-name"

# The Azure OpenAI API version.
#
# DO NOT LEAVE THIS BLANK and do not lower it. The kagent chart's own default is
# 2023-05-15, which PREDATES tool calling: with that version Azure accepts the
# request, silently ignores the tools, and replies in prose. That failure looks
# exactly like a weak model and sends you debugging entirely the wrong thing.
AOAI_API_VERSION="2024-12-01-preview"
